ially - again the  importance of sharks in the ocean right  so everyone know big sharks no fish for  our grandchildren they are the balance  keepers and if they thrive the system  thrives and the point is that as much as  there's so much fear about sharks movies  books everything else the amount we know  about them is still so limited yeah  shocking you know that it was hard  to have access for the scientists  because they were so big and when we  created access by using the lift on the  ship it just exploded science forward on  multiple disciplines and we are learning  fast we are going to win this one we  have 23 sharks tagged in the North  Atlantic right now and another 25 sharks  our show and we will nail down a four  hundred million year old secret and  leverage that data for our grandchildren  and one of those sharks tagged is a  mature female named Lydia what are you  hoping to learn from her  Lydia is the only mature female that we  haven't tagged in Cape Cod or the  Nantucket region we tagged her in  Jacksonville and she's never gone to the  Cape Cod area we believe some of the  Sharks are mating in that Cape Cod area  because other big mature females are  there but what Lydia did is she went to  a small island called Sable Island in  Nova Scotia and we believe that she may  be showing us a secondary mating site  like we see off the west coast at the  Farallon Islands in Guadalupe Island  down in Mexico it's really interesting  what Lydia's doing and Mary Lee is the  shark that you tagged in 2012 lots of  people follow her on Twitter what have  we learned from her so Mary Lee we've  been tracking for five years this  September and really what we've seen is  she is totally shifting the conversation  for sharks around the whole world she's  the most famous real shark in history  and she led us to May and last May to  Long Island which led us to capturing  and tagging the young of the year white  sharks we had Jack Casey did some great  work in the 80s Toby Curtis of NOAA  recently and then Mary lien go in there  we thought maybe they're giving birth  there and we roll it up there we got  nine young of the year pups that were  just a couple years old and since that  time they have defined where they live  in the nursery the first year of their  lives between Long Island and South  Korea  we don't know that for sure we our best  guess is yes we we hear a lot more  stories it seems about shark encounters  with people Nets have been removed there  are more sharks it seems out there  there's always a lot of concern your  t-shirt says don't fear the fin don't  fear the thing what what should people  make of these stories we when you see a  shark near a beach they should be  thrilled that means there's going to be  fish for their children to eat  that means the system's returning toward  abundance you know the fear of sharks is  kind of a real primal thing we all have  but  it's an irrational fear that doesn't  statistically exist here it's more  dangerous to make toast in the morning  but you remind people not to swim  towards the sharp use some common sense  right avoid the evenings in the mornings  if the if the fish are feeding and the  porpoise are there and the seals are  there move to a different place  congressman we'll see you back on the  boat again soon appreciate it yeah  always great to have you here thank you  for having me

